# A `#Test` body is checked the same way by `jet test`, `jet run` and
# `jet build`: a body one of them refuses, the others refuse with the same
# diagnostic. Here the test reads a script's top-level binding, which no
# function can see. `jet test` and `jet build` print it word for word alike.
# A refused `jet run` names each file by its full path (so its report reads
# the same from any directory), so its report is matched line by line around
# that path.
fixture: run_and_test_refuse_a_test_body_alike.fixture
env: NO_COLOR=1
env: TERM=dumb
step: jet test main.jet
exit: 1
stderr: ~ "`total` is a local of this file's top-level statements" # the test body is refused
capture: test_err = text stderr
write: test.err = "{cap:test_err}"
step: jet build main.jet
exit: 1
capture: build_err = text stderr
write: build.err = "{cap:build_err}"
file: build.err == test.err # the same diagnostic, word for word
step: jet run main.jet
exit: 1
stdout: !~ "5" # the script never runs
stderr: ~ "Error [jet.UnknownName]: `total` is a local of this file's top-level statements, which a function can't see\n  --> " # the same headline
stderr: ~ "main.jet:6:15\n    |\n  6 |     assert_eq(total, 5)\n    |               ^^^^^\n Why: A file's top-level statements are its entry, and the names they bind are that entry's locals\n Fix: Pass `total` as a parameter\nMore: jet-lang.dev/e/E0107\n" # the same span, why and fix
